it was built in the late 1970s to  commemorate indonesia's independence  more than two hundred thousand people  can fit inside the istiklal mosque one  of the capital's most iconic buildings  from the outside the mosque has looked  much the same for decades  but sustainability was one of the main  motivations during recent renovations  this mosque is 42 years old and the  equipment had not been replaced for a  long time  as part of the renovation solar panels  were added to the roof for now they only  support a small percentage of the  building's electricity consumption  and thanks to low flow taps and  treatment facilities  water usage has dropped by more than 30  percent  the istiklal mosque's green initiatives  aren't just good for the environment but  for its finances too since its  renovation and its switch to more energy  efficient lighting the mosque's  electricity bills have more than halved  in april the mosque became the first  place of worship in the world to receive  certification for sustainable design  from the international finance  corporation  the renovations cost more than 35  million dollars paid for by the  indonesian government  we worked with the ministry of public  works to realize our vision of an  environmentally friendly mosque  on the outskirts of the capital  worshipers at this mosque started their  own water saving initiatives  with a low-cost innovation which uses  the soles of old shoes to create a  stopper to reduce the flow of water  we have 650  children who who study here about  reading quran we educate also  environment from from the beginning  the mosques management also collects  rubbish from the community so it can be  recycled  people  come to the portsmouth it is easier for  us you know to to influence them and to  educate them instead of we are inviting  people  polluted and sinking fast the indonesian  capital faces many challenges  eco mosques are just part of the effort  to help ease the city's environmental  burden  jessica washington al jazeera jakarta
 

